{"availability":"available","full":"notactuallytreyanastasio/shoehorn","name":"shoehorn","description":"Explore this public GitHub project.","category":"Other","language":"Rust","stars":91,"forks":8,"created":"2026-08-13T19:05:59Z","updated":"2026-08-19T17:41:52Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"https://notactuallytreyanastasio.github.io/shoehorn/","topics":[],"demo":"https://notactuallytreyanastasio.github.io/shoehorn/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-17T12:30:36.592Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-17T12:30:36.592Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-17T12:30:36.592Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T18:00:22.236Z","stars":91,"forks":8},{"at":"2026-10-13T04:14:26.476Z","stars":91,"forks":8},{"at":"2026-10-17T12:30:36.592Z","stars":91,"forks":8}],"technologies":["Rust"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Quantize a BF16 GGUF model with an imatrix so it fits exactly into your available VRAM, then run it with llama.cpp.","shoehorn's site has downloads and a browser-side \"what fits your machine?\" calculator.","Preset quantizations (Q4KM, Q5KS, ...) ignore your hardware. Pick one that fits your machine and you either leave hundreds of megabytes of quality unused or find out at load time that it didn't fit after all. shoehorn starts from the memory you actually have, subtracts what inference itself will need (KV cache, compute buffers), and solves a per-tensor mixed-precision assignment whose total size lands within a rounding error of the remainder. Every spare megabyte goes where the importance matrix says it buys the most model quality."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/notactuallytreyanastasio/shoehorn/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"348183bc150db217112e2fe4c504185183fb93f846ac67e288e371c7a12e52b3","extractedAt":"2026-10-09T18:00:22.457Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-17T12:30:36.693Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Quantize a BF16 GGUF model with an imatrix so it fits exactly int